<.shell title="Error summary" current={:error_summary}>
  <h1>Error summary</h1>
  <p class="lead">What to fix in a form after a failed save.</p>

  <div class="example">
    <.error_summary form={settings_form()} />
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    At the top of a long form after a failed save, as on team settings and in <a href={
      page_path(:fix_form_errors)
    }>Fix form errors</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <p>A short form, where every error is in view. The error on the field is enough.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The title is always "There is a problem".</li>
    <li>Each line is the field's error, word for word, and links to the field.</li>
    <li>It shows after a save, not while people type.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.error_summary form=&#123;@form&#125; /&gt;</code>
    first inside the <code>&lt;.form&gt;</code>. It reads the changeset's errors and renders nothing without them.
  </p>
</.shell>
